---
import {
  EAGER_TILES,
  FULL_SIZES,
  FULL_WIDTHS,
  GALLERY_COPY,
  GRID_SIZES,
  GRID_WIDTHS,
  galleryPosition,
} from '../lib/gallery';
import { localeUrl, type Locale } from '../lib/i18n';
import {
  imageFocus,
  imageSize,
  imageSrc,
  imageSrcSet,
  type GalleryImage,
} from '../lib/sanity';

/**
 * `/gallery` — the photographs, a lightbox, and a page that says plainly there are none
 * (MUSE-25).
 *
 * =================================================================================
 * ## 1. The empty state is the shipped state, and this page is the honest hard case
 *
 * No photography of this studio exists. `src/lib/gallery.ts` carries the full argument;
 * the part that is this file's is what the markup does about it:
 *
 *   - **no grid, no section heading and no lede** when `photographs` is empty. The lede is
 *     the one thing this page withholds that `/events` does not, and the reason is the
 *     ticket's own: an events page with no events still has a subject, a gallery with no
 *     photographs does not, so a lede here would be a sentence describing pictures that do
 *     not exist;
 *   - **no filler.** No placeholder tiles, no decorative frames, no stock imagery. A grid
 *     of grey squares is MUSE-36's invented schedule with the words taken out;
 *   - **somewhere else to go** — the timetable, which is what the studio actually runs, and
 *     the contact page.
 *
 * ## 2. The lightbox: a native `<dialog>`, opened by an inline script
 *
 * The ticket's criteria are arrow keys, Escape, **focus trapped inside and returned to the
 * thumbnail it opened from**, over a plum-ink 96% backdrop in both themes — and the built
 * output contains zero `.js` files (`test/nojs.test.ts`), so whatever mechanism this is has
 * to be CSS, or markup, or an inline `<script>`. Three were available. The choice is
 * `showModal()` and the reason is one word: **inertness.**
 *
 *   - **`:target`** is the pure-CSS construction and it fails the criteria rather than
 *     approximating them. It cannot trap focus at all — every link, every nav item and
 *     every other tile behind the overlay stays in the tab order and in the accessibility
 *     tree — it cannot bind an arrow key, and it writes to session history: browsing twelve
 *     photographs leaves twelve entries, so Back stops meaning „the page I came from" on
 *     the one page a visitor is most likely to arrive at from a search result. A gallery
 *     whose Back button is spent is worse than one with no enlargement.
 *   - **`popover`** is tempting because `popovertarget` needs no script at all: it opens,
 *     Escape light-dismisses, and focus returns to the invoker, all from the platform. But
 *     `popover="auto"` **does not make the rest of the document inert** — it is explicitly
 *     specified not to — so Tab walks straight out of the „modal" into the page behind it.
 *     That is the one criterion this ticket singles out as the thing modals get wrong, and
 *     it would be wrong in the quietest possible way: looks modal, reads modal, is not.
 *   - **`<dialog>` + `showModal()`** makes every other element in the document inert by
 *     specification. Tab cycles inside the dialog because there is nowhere else to go, not
 *     because a keydown handler counted the focusables and wrapped the index — and a
 *     hand-rolled trap is a loop that is wrong the day somebody adds a control, or the day
 *     a browser changes what is focusable. Escape fires `cancel` and closes. Closing
 *     **returns focus to the element that was focused when `showModal()` was called**,
 *     which is the thumbnail, from the platform. Four of the ticket's criteria, none of
 *     them our arithmetic.
 *
 * The script adds exactly what the platform does not have an opinion about: which
 * photograph is showing, the arrow keys, the counter, and click-to-dismiss.
 *
 * ### What happens with JavaScript off
 *
 * **The grid is the gallery, and the tile is a link to the photograph.** Every picture is
 * on the page, at the size the grid paints it, with its alt text, with no mechanism
 * involved. Each tile is an `<a href>` pointing at that photograph's own 1200w URL on
 * Sanity's CDN, so activating it opens the picture in the browser's own image view — which
 * pans, zooms, is keyboard-operable and has a working Back button. The script
 * `preventDefault`s a plain left-click or Enter and opens the dialog instead; a
 * middle-click or ⌘/Ctrl-click is deliberately left alone, so „open the photograph in a new
 * tab" keeps working (MUSE-33's case).
 *
 * That is a single mechanism of ours with the platform underneath it, which is the
 * property being bought. The rejected alternative was a `:target` panel *beneath* the
 * dialog so that a no-script visitor got a lightbox too — and it is rejected for MUSE-38's
 * reason: two individually-correct mechanisms answering one question between them is how
 * that defect happened, `npm run a11y` runs with scripting on so only one of the two would
 * ever be audited, and the unaudited one would be the one that cannot trap focus. Offering
 * a no-script visitor a half-modal is offering them this ticket's own failure mode.
 *
 * The cost, stated: a no-script visitor gets no counter, no arrow navigation and no
 * in-page enlargement, and the `<dialog>`'s markup — ~6 KB of `srcset` on a twelve-picture
 * page — is weight they never use. A static site cannot render that conditionally.
 *
 * ### Why the frames are all here, and all `hidden`
 *
 * One dialog holding one `<figure>` per photograph, rather than one dialog whose `src` the
 * script rewrites. Rewriting means the responsive URL ladder exists twice — once in
 * `imageSrcSet` and once in JavaScript — and the copy in JavaScript is the one no test can
 * see. Holding them all means the index is a `hidden` toggle and the URLs stay in the read
 * path where `test/projections.test.ts` already runs them.
 *
 * `hidden` is load-bearing beyond display: a `loading="lazy"` image inside a
 * `display: none` subtree has no box, so it never intersects the viewport and is **never
 * fetched**. A closed dialog therefore costs zero bytes, and only the photograph being
 * looked at is downloaded. The script primes the two neighbours on each move — it clears
 * `loading="lazy"` rather than showing them — so an arrow press paints from cache instead
 * of starting a request.
 *
 * ## 3. The ratio is CSS's and the width is the CDN's (§9 rule 2)
 *
 * `src/lib/sanity/images.ts` has the long argument. Here it means the tile is a square
 * frame with `aspect-ratio: 1` in the stylesheet — which is render-blocking, so the box is
 * reserved before a single photograph arrives and the grid cannot reflow as they load —
 * with `object-fit: cover` and `object-position` on the hotspot. The lightbox frame is the
 * opposite: the photograph's own ratio, so its `width`/`height` attributes come from
 * `imageSize` and the box is reserved from the file's real dimensions.
 *
 * **The design system's two sections disagree about the grid and §9 wins.** §7.4 says
 * „mixed aspect ratios"; §9 rule 4 lists `1:1` as the gallery ratio, the Studio's own field
 * description promises Mina her upload is cropped to 1:1, and the ticket says „images are
 * 1:1 in the grid; the lightbox shows the original ratio". Three to one, and the one is the
 * only place in either document where a masonry layout is implied — with no column
 * algorithm, no gutter behaviour and no ordering rule to go with it. Noted rather than
 * silently resolved: if mixed ratios are wanted, that is a design decision with a layout
 * attached and it needs the hotspot story re-thought, because a hotspot is what makes one
 * upload survive four crops.
 * =================================================================================
 */
interface Props {
  locale: Locale;
  /**
   * The photographs, from `getGallery({ minimum: 0 })`. **No default.**
   *
   * `Schedule.astro`'s rule (MUSE-36): a default is what makes it possible to render a page
   * before anybody has asked the studio what is on it. An empty array is a legitimate value
   * and is the value this page ships with; what is not legitimate is this component having
   * an opinion about what the dataset contains.
   */
  photographs: GalleryImage[];
}

const { locale, photographs } = Astro.props;
const t = GALLERY_COPY[locale];

/**
 * Every URL and every box, resolved once per photograph.
 *
 * In the frontmatter rather than inline in the markup so that the grid tile and the
 * lightbox frame for one photograph are visibly two renderings of one `ImageRef` — and so
 * that `alt` is read from the dataset exactly once per surface. An `alt` resolved twice is
 * an `alt` that can be resolved differently.
 */
const tiles = photographs.map((photograph, index) => {
  const ref = photograph.image;
  return {
    index,
    alt: ref.alt[locale],
    caption: photograph.caption?.[locale],
    focus: imageFocus(ref),
    // The middle rung is the `src`: the fallback for a browser that ignores `srcset`, and
    // never the largest file in the set.
    thumb: imageSrc(ref, { width: GRID_WIDTHS[1]! }),
    thumbSet: imageSrcSet(ref, GRID_WIDTHS),
    full: imageSrc(ref, { width: FULL_WIDTHS[1]! }),
    fullSet: imageSrcSet(ref, FULL_WIDTHS),
    // From the widest rung, so the declared box is the one the largest file really has —
    // `imageSize` clamps to the asset, so it cannot promise pixels the upload lacks.
    box: imageSize(ref, { width: FULL_WIDTHS[FULL_WIDTHS.length - 1]! }),
  };
});
---

<section class="hero">
  <p class="eyebrow">{t.eyebrow}</p>
  <h1 class="display">{t.heading}</h1>
  {/* Only beside a populated grid — see the note at the top of this file. */}
  {tiles.length > 0 && <p class="lede">{t.lede}</p>}
</section>

{
  tiles.length > 0 ? (
    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="sectionH">{t.photographs}</h2>
        <ul class="grid">
          {tiles.map((tile) => (
            <li class="cell">
              {/*
                A link, not a button, and the href is the photograph. That is what makes the
                no-script state a working gallery rather than a dead affordance — see „What
                happens with JavaScript off" above. The accessible name is the `alt`, so
                there is deliberately no `aria-label` here: one would override it and
                announce „Enlarge" twelve times in place of twelve descriptions.
              */}
              <a class="tile" href={tile.full} data-tile={tile.index}>
                <img
                  src={tile.thumb}
                  srcset={tile.thumbSet}
                  sizes={GRID_SIZES}
                  alt={tile.alt}
                  loading={tile.index < EAGER_TILES ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
                  decoding="async"
                  style={`object-position: ${tile.focus.x}% ${tile.focus.y}%`}
                />
              </a>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  ) : (
    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="emptyH">{t.emptyHeading}</h2>
        <p class="emptyP">{t.emptyBody}</p>
        <p class="exits">
          <a class="exit" href={localeUrl('/schedule', locale)}>
            {t.toSchedule}
          </a>
          <a class="exit" href={localeUrl('/contact', locale)}>
            {t.toContact}
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  )
}

{
  /*
    The lightbox. Rendered only when there is something to enlarge: an empty `<dialog>`
    is markup nothing can open, and `data-lightbox` is what the script looks for, so an
    empty gallery runs none of the behaviour below.

    Control order is DOM order and DOM order is focus order. `showModal()` focuses the
    first tabbable child, so Close is first: it is the one control whose effect a visitor
    who pressed Enter by reflex already wanted, and it is where every other modal on the
    web puts initial focus.
  */
  tiles.length > 0 && (
    <dialog class="lightbox" data-lightbox aria-label={t.lightbox}>
      <button class="close" type="button" data-close aria-label={t.close}>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <path d="M6 6l12 12M18 6L6 18" />
        </svg>
      </button>

      <div class="stage">
        {tiles.map((tile) => (
          <figure class="frame" data-frame={tile.index} hidden>
            <img
              src={tile.full}
              srcset={tile.fullSet}
              sizes={FULL_SIZES}
              alt={tile.alt}
              width={tile.box.width}
              height={tile.box.height}
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
            {tile.caption !== undefined && <figcaption class="caption">{tile.caption}</figcaption>}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="controls">
        <button class="step" type="button" data-prev aria-label={t.previous}>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M14 5l-7 7 7 7" />
          </svg>
        </button>
        {/*
          `role="status"` so a move is announced. The visible half is the counter §7.4 asks
          for; the hidden half is the new photograph's own description, because a reader
          who hears „3 / 12" and nothing else has been told their position and not what
          they are looking at. Both are set by the script, and the figures are lining and
          tabular from the document — this component must not redeclare
          `font-variant-numeric` to get them (MUSE-14).
        */}
        <p class="counter" role="status">
          <span data-count>{galleryPosition(0, tiles.length)}</span>
          <span class="srOnly" data-described></span>
        </p>
        <button class="step" type="button" data-next aria-label={t.next}>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M10 5l7 7-7 7" />
          </svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  )
}

<style>
  /* §3 section rhythm: canvas, then one contrasting surface. Not alternating. */
  .hero {
    max-width: 1280px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-5) var(--space-6);
  }
  .band {
    padding: var(--space-7) var(--space-5) var(--space-8);
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .hero {
      padding: var(--space-10) var(--space-7) var(--space-7);
    }
    .band {
      padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-7) var(--space-10);
    }
  }
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1280px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.32em;
    /* Č Ć Ž Š carry marks above the cap line — 1.4 is the floor in Croatian (§4.6). */
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* Cormorant, so never below 26px — `clamp()`'s first value is the floor (§4). */
  .display {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(2.75rem, 8vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 0.96;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    text-wrap: balance;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lede {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-5);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    /* §5: a measure rather than a width, so Croatian — 20–25% longer than English —
       reflows instead of being clipped (§10). */
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .sectionH {
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  /* --- the grid: 2 / 3 / 4 columns, 8px gutters (§7.4) --- */
  /*
    `repeat(N, 1fr)` and never `auto-fit`. With three photographs in a four-column grid,
    `1fr` tracks keep each tile one quarter wide and leave the fourth column empty, which
    is the ticket's „fewer than a full row does not stretch them to fill"; `auto-fit` with
    `minmax()` would spread three photographs across the full width and silently change
    their crop. The gutter is `--space-2`, which is §5's 8px.
  */
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: var(--space-2);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
    list-style: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    }
  }
  /* No min-width anywhere in the grid: a pixel floor on a track is what overflows 390px. */
  .cell {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /*
    The tile. §7.4: „hover raises to full opacity from 88%" — so the resting state is 0.88
    and the raise is on hover *and* focus-visible, because a keyboard visitor moving along
    the grid is doing the same thing a pointer is. No scale and no shadow: §5 depth is
    surface value plus a hairline, in both themes.
  */
  .tile {
    display: block;
    /* The box, reserved by the stylesheet rather than by the file (§9 rule 2). */
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    /* Plum in both themes: this is the ground a photograph loads onto, and a cream frame
       would fight the picture (§9). `--band-surface` is the role set for a surface that
       does not follow the page theme. */
    background: var(--band-surface);
    opacity: 0.88;
    transition: opacity var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
  }
  .tile:hover,
  .tile:focus-visible {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  .tile img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* --- the empty state --- */
  /*
    display-s, clamped at Cormorant's 26px floor — the smallest display size there is (§4).
    A sentence rather than a label: this is the page's answer to the question the visitor
    arrived with, so it is set at the size an answer is set at.
  */
  .emptyH {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(1.625rem, 3vw, 2.125rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .emptyP {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-4);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }
  .exits {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-6);
  }
  .exit {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    /* 44px is the §8 touch target. */
    min-height: 44px;
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
  }

  /* --- the lightbox (§7.4) --- */
  /*
    The scrim is the dialog's own background and `::backdrop` is cleared, which is the one
    place this file departs from the obvious reading of §7.4 („a plum-ink 96% backdrop").
    Two reasons, and they are mechanical rather than aesthetic:

      1. **Custom properties do not reliably reach `::backdrop`.** Inheriting from the
         originating element is a recent change to the spec and a recent change in
         implementations, so `background: color-mix(…, var(--band-surface) …)` there
         resolves to nothing on a browser a year old — a fully transparent overlay, with
         the page legible underneath and nothing failing. The alternative is a literal
         plum-ink, which `npm run ds` refuses for the reason that rule exists.
      2. **A click has to be able to dismiss.** `::backdrop` is not an event target. With
         the dialog itself filling the viewport, a click outside the photograph lands on
         the dialog element and the script can read it.

    `::backdrop` is set transparent rather than left alone because the UA default for a
    modal dialog is a translucent black, which would sit under this and darken the brand
    colour by an amount nobody chose.
  */
  .lightbox {
    /* Set under `[open]` only: `display` here would override the UA rule that keeps a
       closed dialog — and all of its photographs — out of the layout entirely. */
    inline-size: 100vw;
    max-inline-size: 100vw;
    block-size: 100dvh;
    max-block-size: 100dvh;
    margin: 0;
    /* 12px, not 24: §7.4 gives the photograph 90vw, and 24 either side leaves only 342 of
       a 390px phone for an image entitled to 351. The scrim is the dialog's own
       background, so the padding is breathing room rather than structure. */
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border: 0;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--band-surface) 96%, transparent);
    color: var(--band-text);
  }
  .lightbox::backdrop {
    background: transparent;
  }
  /*
    Two rows: the photograph, then the controls. `minmax(0, 1fr)` rather than `1fr`,
    because a `1fr` track has an automatic **minimum** of its content — so a tall
    photograph makes the row taller than the viewport instead of being asked to shrink,
    and the overflow is then centred, which puts the top of the picture off the top of the
    screen. That is the whole of this block: every box between the dialog and the `<img>`
    carries `min-block-size: 0`, so „the image fits in what is left" is a chain with no
    break in it. Measured at 390 × 844 and 1280 × 900, both themes.
  */
  .lightbox[open] {
    display: grid;
    /* One bounded column: an `auto` column is sized by its contents, so the stage could
       make it wider than the viewport and then centre the overflow — which is a
       photograph half off the right edge of a phone, with the document's own scroll width
       unchanged because a modal dialog lives in the top layer. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
    justify-items: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    animation: lightboxIn var(--dur-base) var(--ease);
  }
  @keyframes lightboxIn {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }
  /*
    §8: under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` the fade is gone, not shortened. The global
    rule in `src/styles/base.css` collapses every duration to 0.01ms, which is already
    enough — this is here because an overlay appearing is the one transition on this page a
    motion-sensitive visitor meets, and „it is handled somewhere else" is how a rule like
    that stops being true.
  */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .lightbox[open] {
      animation: none;
    }
  }

  /*
    **The sizing chain, which is the only fiddly thing in this file — and it is arithmetic
    rather than percentages, deliberately.**

    The obvious construction is a `minmax(0, 1fr)` row with `max-block-size: 100%` on the
    image, so the picture fits „whatever is left" with no number in it. It does not work
    here and the reason is worth writing down: the image's *width* is `auto`, so the row's
    height depends on the image, and the image's height then depends on the row — a cyclic
    percentage, which Chromium resolves by treating the percentage as indefinite and
    leaving the picture at a hundred-odd pixels. Measured, three ways round, at both
    viewports.

    So the ceiling is viewport arithmetic. `85vh` is §7.4's own number; `100dvh - 12rem`
    is what is left after the chrome beneath the photograph, and 12rem = 192px is the sum
    of the parts rather than a guess: the controls row is 44, the caption up to two lines
    is 46, the two gaps are 12 and 16, and the dialog's padding is 24 top and bottom. That
    is 166, and the 26 left over is the third caption line a long Croatian sentence can
    take. `dvh` and not `vh` because a phone's toolbar moves the viewport and `vh` does
    not notice.
  */
  .stage {
    display: grid;
    /* Bounded here too, and for the same reason one level up: an `auto` column takes the
       photograph's max-content width, so the frame was centred inside a column wider than
       the phone and the picture sat 13px off the right edge. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    justify-items: center;
    align-content: center;
    inline-size: 100%;
    min-block-size: 0;
  }
  .frame {
    display: grid;
    justify-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    min-block-size: 0;
  }
  /*
    **This line is load-bearing and its absence is silent.** `[hidden] { display: none }`
    is a *user-agent* rule, and any author `display` beats the user-agent stylesheet
    whatever the specificity — so the rule above quietly put all seven photographs in the
    layout at once, each one a full-size image the browser then fetched the moment the
    lightbox opened. Measured before this line: a stage 5,002px tall inside a 900px
    viewport, with the photograph that was asked for 636px above the top of the screen.
    Nothing about the markup was wrong; `hidden` was simply not doing anything.
    `test/gallery.test.ts` asserts the layout box count with the lightbox open.
  */
  .frame[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }
  /* §7.4: the photograph at up to 90vw by 85vh, at its own ratio. */
  .frame img {
    display: block;
    max-inline-size: 90vw;
    max-block-size: min(85vh, calc(100dvh - 12rem));
    inline-size: auto;
    block-size: auto;
    object-fit: contain;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .caption {
    /* `90vw`, not a bare 62ch measure: 62ch is wider than a 390px phone, and a caption box
       wider than its frame pushes the photograph off centre rather than wrapping. */
    max-inline-size: min(62ch, 90vw);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--band-muted);
  }

  .controls {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }
  /* §7.4: prev / next as accent hairline circles. 44px is the §8 touch target. */
  .step,
  .close {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    inline-size: 44px;
    block-size: 44px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--band-line-accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    color: var(--band-accent);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition:
      border-color var(--dur-fast) var(--ease),
      opacity var(--dur-fast) var(--ease);
  }
  .step:hover,
  .close:hover {
    border-color: var(--band-accent);
  }
  .step svg,
  .close svg {
    inline-size: 20px;
    block-size: 20px;
    fill: none;
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-linecap: round;
  }
  /*
    Top-right, out of the photograph's way. `position: fixed` rather than `absolute`: the
    dialog is in the top layer and fills the viewport, so the two agree, and `fixed` keeps
    the button put if the stage ever scrolls.
  */
  .close {
    position: fixed;
    inset-block-start: var(--space-4);
    inset-inline-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  /* §7.4: the counter in `caption`. */
  .counter {
    min-inline-size: 5ch;
    font-family: var(--font-label);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.2em;
    line-height: 1.4;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--band-muted);
  }

  .srOnly {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * The lightbox, and nothing the platform already does.
   *
   * Locale-agnostic on purpose, `TrialForm.astro`'s rule: every string this needs is
   * already in the markup, so the script reads the DOM instead of being handed copy. That
   * is what keeps it one bundled script for both locales rather than two.
   *
   * What is deliberately absent: a focus trap, an Escape handler, and a focus-return. All
   * three are `showModal()`'s, by specification — see the long note in the frontmatter.
   * Writing them here would be writing a second, worse answer beside a working one.
   */
  const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('dialog[data-lightbox]');

  // `showModal` is the whole mechanism, so a browser without it gets the no-script page:
  // the tiles stay plain links to the photographs. Feature-detected rather than assumed,
  // because the alternative is a tile whose click is cancelled and replaced by nothing.
  if (dialog && typeof dialog.showModal === 'function') {
    const frames = [...dialog.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-frame]')];
    const tiles = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-tile]')];
    const count = dialog.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-count]');
    const described = dialog.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-described]');
    const total = frames.length;
    let at = 0;

    /**
     * Let a neighbour start loading without showing it.
     *
     * A `loading="lazy"` image in a `hidden` subtree has no box, never intersects the
     * viewport and is never fetched — which is what makes a closed dialog cost nothing.
     * Clearing the attribute turns that into an ordinary eager load, so the next arrow
     * press paints from cache. Showing them instead would fetch all twelve on open.
     */
    const prime = (index: number) => {
      const image = frames[index]?.querySelector('img');
      if (image) image.loading = 'eager';
    };

    const show = (index: number) => {
      at = (index + total) % total;
      frames.forEach((frame, position) => {
        frame.hidden = position !== at;
      });
      if (count) count.textContent = `${at + 1} / ${total}`;
      // The description, for the `role="status"` region: position alone says where you are
      // and not what you are looking at.
      const image = frames[at]?.querySelector('img');
      if (described && image) described.textContent = image.alt;
      prime(at);
      prime((at + 1) % total);
      prime((at - 1 + total) % total);
    };

    tiles.forEach((tile, index) => {
      tile.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
        // A modified click is the visitor asking for a new tab or a download, and the href
        // is the photograph, so the honest thing is to let the browser have it (MUSE-33).
        if (event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) return;
        if (event.button !== 0) return;
        event.preventDefault();
        show(index);
        dialog.showModal();
      });
    });

    dialog.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
      // Escape is the platform's, and `cancel` fires before this would see it.
      const steps: Record<string, number | undefined> = {
        ArrowRight: at + 1,
        ArrowLeft: at - 1,
        Home: 0,
        End: total - 1,
      };
      const next = steps[event.key];
      if (next === undefined) return;
      event.preventDefault();
      show(next);
    });

    dialog.querySelector('[data-prev]')?.addEventListener('click', () => show(at - 1));
    dialog.querySelector('[data-next]')?.addEventListener('click', () => show(at + 1));
    dialog.querySelector('[data-close]')?.addEventListener('click', () => dialog.close());

    // A click on the scrim. The dialog fills the viewport, so anything that is not the
    // photograph, the caption or a control lands on the dialog element itself.
    dialog.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (event.target === dialog) dialog.close();
    });
  }
</script>
